{
  "ID": "2.1",
  "Context/Background": "ASXL1 plays key roles in epigenetic regulation of early developmental gene expression. De novo protein-truncating mutations in ASXL1 cause Bohring-Opitz syndrome (BOS), a rare neurodevelopmental condition characterized by severe intellectual disabilities, distinctive facial features, hypertrichosis, increased risk of Wilms tumor, and variable congenital anomalies, including heart defects and severe skeletal defects giving rise to a typical BOS posture. These BOS-causing ASXL1 variants are also high-prevalence somatic driver mutations in acute myeloid leukemia.  We used primary cells from individuals with BOS (n = 18) and controls (n = 49) to dissect gene regulatory changes caused by ASXL1 mutations using comprehensive multiomics assays for chromatin accessibility (ATAC-seq), DNA methylation, histone methylation binding, and transcriptome in peripheral blood and skin fibroblasts.\n",
  "Prompt": "\nBased on the results of the blood and fibroblast transcriptomic DEG analyses of BOS vs controls, provide a discussion of the common and distinct pathways found between blood and fibroblast and any relevance to the presence in the BOS samples of ASXL1 truncating mutations.",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "nvidia",
  "llm_model": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b",
  "run_date": "2026-01-21 14:33:53",
  "elapsed_seconds": 175.86,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "**Introduction/Context**  \nASXL1 truncating mutations cause Bohring-Opitz syndrome (BOS), characterized by neurodevelopmental defects, skeletal anomalies, and cancer predisposition. Transcriptomic analysis of blood and fibroblasts from BOS patients revealed distinct and shared pathways.  \n\n**Keywords**  \n- ASXL1 truncating mutations  \n- Hedgehog signaling  \n- Hematopoietic lineage  \n- Wnt/PCP signaling  \n\n**Candidate Genes**  \n- *VANGL2* (Wnt/planar cell polarity)  \n- *GRIK5* (glutamate receptor)  \n- *CHRM3* (acetylcholine receptor)  \n- *CR2* (complement receptor)  \n\n**Pathway Enrichments**  \n**Fibroblasts (ORA):**  \n- **Upregulated:**  \n  - **Insulin secretion** (KEGG): *CHRM3, SNAP25, KCNMB4, CACNA1D* (P=5.17×10⁻⁴, OR=11.8)  \n  - *No significant downregulated pathways at FDR<0.05*  \n\n**Blood (ORA):**  \n- **Upregulated:**  \n  - **Hematopoietic cell lineage** (KEGG): *CR2, CD8B, ITGA1, DNTT* (P=3.89×10⁻⁵, OR=8.38)  \n  - **Hedgehog signaling** (Hallmark): *ACHE, CRMP1, NRCAM* (P=4.51×10⁻³, OR=9.83)  \n  - **ECM-receptor interaction** (KEGG): *COL6A2, ITGA1, HSPG2* (P=1.38×10⁻³, OR=6.57)  \n  - *Protein digestion/absorption* (KEGG): *SLC6A19, COL6A2, SLC16A10* (P=2.77×10⁻³, OR=5.56)  \n\n**Common Pathways**  \n- *VANGL2* (Wnt/PCP signaling) and *GRIK5* (glutamate signaling) were upregulated in both tissues.  \n\n**Targeted Enrichment Analysis**  \n- **Hedgehog signaling** (blood) and **insulin secretion** (fibroblasts) were enriched, aligning with ASXL1’s role in developmental gene regulation.  \n- **Hematopoietic lineage** (blood) may link to BOS’s Wilms tumor risk.  \n\n**Key Genes/Proteins**  \n**Upregulated:**  \n| Gene      | Direction | Role/Function                     | Pathway(s)                |  \n|-----------|-----------|-----------------------------------|---------------------------|  \n| *VANGL2*  | ↑↑        | Wnt/PCP signaling, skeletal development | Hedgehog, ECM interaction |  \n| *GRIK5*   | ↑         | Glutamate receptor, neurodevelopment | Myogenesis, Hedgehog      |  \n| *CR2*     | ↑         | B-cell activation, immune response | Hematopoietic lineage     |  \n\n**Comparisons**  \n- Blood showed stronger enrichment for developmental (Hedgehog) and immune (hematopoietic) pathways, while fibroblasts highlighted metabolic (insulin) dysregulation.  \n- Shared *VANGL2* upregulation suggests conserved disruption of Wnt/PCP signaling in BOS.  \n\n**Implications**  \n- ASXL1 loss may derepress developmental pathways (e.g., Hedgehog/Wnt) and immune/metabolic genes, driving BOS’s pleiotropy.  \n- Hematopoietic lineage enrichment in blood supports ASXL1’s role in leukemia predisposition.  \n\n**Next Steps**  \n1. Validate ASXL1 binding to promoter regions of *VANGL2*, *GRIK5* via ChIP-seq.  \n2. Investigate Hedgehog/Wnt crosstalk in ASXL1-mutant models.  \n3. Assess insulin signaling in BOS patient fibroblasts metabolically.  \n\n**References**  \n- PMID 300459 (TNMD’s role in skeletal development)  \n- PMID 600283 (GRIK5 in neurodevelopmental disorders)  \n- PMID 607280 (CNTN4’s association with Wilms tumor)  \n\nThis analysis highlights tissue-specific and shared pathways in BOS, implicating ASXL1 in epigenetic control of development and immunity.",
